# ZoFHub — environment configuration TEMPLATE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in your REAL values.
#   cp .env.example .env
# NEVER commit your real .env — it is gitignored on purpose. This template
# contains placeholders ONLY (no real keys) and is safe to commit.
# In production (Render/Railway/Fly), set these in the host's dashboard, not here.
#
# Every variable the app reads is listed below. Required ones have no "#";
# optional ones are commented out with their default noted.
# =============================================================================

# ── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
HOST=0.0.0.0
NODE_ENV=development
# Don't auto-open a browser tab when the server starts (also auto-skipped when
# NODE_ENV=production). Set to 1 for headless / server runs.
# NO_OPEN=1

# ZoFHub is ALWAYS a proxy site with proxy-friendly ads — there is no hub /
# Google-AdSense mode. The web proxy is always on; monetization is configured
# per network, one role each (see the ADVERTISING section below).

# ── Supabase (optional but recommended) ──────────────────────────────────
# If unset, the catalog falls back to bundled JSON and Community is disabled.
# Get these from: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys.
#
# NOTE: NONE of these ever reach the browser. This app is an Express monolith
# and the browser talks only to this server's own /api routes — there is no
# client-side Supabase SDK anywhere in public/, by design. So the publishable
# key below is used SERVER-SIDE too (as the read-only fallback when no admin key
# is set); it is not injected into any page. A test asserts that no configured
# secret value appears in any HTTP response body.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
# Public (browser-safe) key — protected by Row Level Security. Supabase now
# labels this the "publishable" key (sb_publishable_…); older projects call it
# the "anon" key (a long eyJ… JWT).
# The app accepts EITHER name — set whichever your dashboard shows. (render.yaml
# uses SUPABASE_ANON_KEY; this file uses SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY — same value.)
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key        # alias for the line above — pick one
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Bypasses RLS for cron + writes. NEVER expose to the browser
# and NEVER commit it.
#
# PREFERRED — the new-style secret key from Settings → API Keys (sb_secret_…).
# Supabase is retiring the legacy `service_role` JWT at the end of 2026, and
# legacy keys can no longer be rotated individually: the only way to retire a
# leaked one is to create a secret key, move onto it, then deactivate the legacy
# pair. A secret key bypasses RLS exactly like service_role, so it's a drop-in.
# When both are set this one wins, and it is also mirrored onto
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY so the CLI tools that read that name keep working.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_your-secret-key
# LEGACY name — DO NOT SET. The anon/service_role pair is deactivated on this
# project; presenting an old eyJ… key returns 401 "Legacy API keys are disabled".
# lib/load-env.mjs mirrors SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY onto this name, which is the only
# reason the CLI tools that read it still work. Setting a stale value here just
# reintroduces a dead credential — the server warns at boot if you do.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# (NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL / _ANON_KEY / _PUBLISHABLE_KEY are also accepted as
#  fallbacks, for configs imported from a Next.js project.)

# ── Leaderboard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The leaderboard is back (it was retired once; see db/scores-restore.sql).
# Scores are REPORTED BY PLAYERS — every game is a third-party cross-origin
# iframe, so there is no way to read a real score out of one. The defences that
# make a fake score cost something are all server-side and all tunable here.
#
# Nothing below is a secret; they are thresholds. The old SCORE_SECRET is gone
# for good — writes are authorised by a single-use proof-of-play token issued
# per game view, not by a shared string.
#
# The feature needs the `scores` table. Until it exists the site degrades
# quietly: the board shows "not switched on" and submissions are refused with a
# plain message. To switch it on, run db/scores-restore.sql in the SQL editor.
#
# SCORE_MAX=1000000000            # absolute ceiling; anything higher is quarantined
# SCORE_MIN_PLAY_MS=8000          # a score submitted sooner than this is quarantined
# SCORE_SESSION_TTL_MS=21600000   # how long a play token stays valid (6h)
# SCORE_OUTLIER_FACTOR=10         # quarantine above N× the best score on the board
# SCORES_KEEP_PER_GAME=200        # rows kept per game by the nightly prune
# SCORE_SESSION_MAX_PER_OWNER=2000 # play tokens one address may hold at once (a
#                                 # school NAT is many people, so this is high)

# ── Mirror-update list (censorship insurance) ────────────────────────────
# POST /api/subscribe stores an address in `subscriber_emails` — two columns,
# no IP, no timestamp (db/14_subscriber_emails.sql). That schema is what makes
# the homepage's "zero tracking" true, so resist adding columns to it.
#
# The table alone stores addresses; these two let you actually SEND to them.
# With both unset the endpoint still works and still stores the row — the
# provider hand-off is skipped, so you can open an account later and lose
# nothing. The key is read only on the server and is never sent to a browser.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_key_here
# RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=your-audience-uuid


# ── Admin console ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are exactly TWO ways to be an administrator, and no third.
#
#   1. A SESSION, for humans — username + password at /admin/login.
#   2. A BEARER TOKEN, for machines — ADMIN_SECRET in an Authorization header.
#
# Generate all three session credentials in one step:
#
#     npm run admin-hash
#
# It prompts for a password with the echo hidden, prints the HASH and a fresh
# session secret, and never writes the password anywhere. Paste the output into
# your host's dashboard (Render → Environment) — preferably not into this file,
# so rotating a credential is not a git commit.
ADMIN_USERNAME=
# scrypt hash produced by `npm run admin-hash`. NEVER a plaintext password.
# Format: scrypt$N$r$p$salt$hash — versioned, so the KDF can be changed later.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Keys the session tokens AND the IP hashes in the audit trail. 32+ chars.
# Changing it signs every admin session out and makes older IP hashes
# incomparable with new ones — which is exactly what you want after a leak.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
#
# Optional session tuning (defaults shown):
# ADMIN_SESSION_MAX_MS=28800000     # absolute lifetime, 8h
# ADMIN_SESSION_IDLE_MS=1800000     # idle timeout, 30m
# ADMIN_LOCK_THRESHOLD=5            # failures from one IP before lockout
# ADMIN_LOCK_WINDOW_MS=900000       # window those failures are counted in
# ADMIN_LOCK_MS=900000              # how long the lockout lasts
#
# The three above guard the PASSWORD login and are backed by the database. The
# three below guard every OTHER way of presenting a credential — chiefly the
# ADMIN_SECRET bearer token — and are in-memory, so they still apply when the
# database is unreachable. Only a credential that was presented AND wrong counts,
# so an anonymous visitor hitting /admin can never lock the operator out.
# ADMIN_CRED_THRESHOLD=8            # wrong credentials from one IP before lockout
# ADMIN_CRED_WINDOW_MS=600000       # window those failures are counted in
# ADMIN_CRED_LOCK_MS=900000         # how long that lockout lasts
#
# ROTATING THE PASSWORD: run `npm run admin-hash` again and replace
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH. Existing sessions stay valid until they expire, so if the
# old password may be known, also press "Sign out everywhere" in the Security
# Centre — that revokes every session server-side, immediately.
#
# LOCKED OUT? The credentials live only in the environment, so there is nothing
# to reset in the database: set a new ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH in your host's
# dashboard and redeploy. ADMIN_SECRET below is the independent break-glass path
# to the admin API while you do it.
#
# MACHINE TOKEN — for uptime monitors and CLI tooling that cannot hold a cookie.
# Sent as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` or the `x-admin-secret` header.
# It is NOT accepted in a query string: a credential in a URL is written to the
# hosting access log, kept in browser history, and forwarded in the Referer of
# every outbound link on the page.
# Auth FAILS CLOSED — while every admin credential is unset, /api/admin/* refuses
# every request, so the catalog, analytics, security and feedback panels are all
# unreachable. Use a long random value, different from every other secret here.
ADMIN_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# GameFlare verification preview token. LEAVE UNSET unless you are running
# `npm run gameflare:verify`, which generates its own ephemeral value anyway.
# When set, the player route will resolve an UNVERIFIED candidate for the browser
# worker; when unset (the default) that path does not exist at all.
# GAMEFLARE_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ADVERTISING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  ZoFHub supports exactly SIX networks, and each one owns exactly ONE role.
#  Nothing else is wired up, and Google AdSense/AdX is refused at three separate
#  layers (Google bans proxy domains at the ACCOUNT level — one stray tag can
#  kill an unrelated AdSense account). Source of truth: lib/ad-providers.mjs.
#
#   Network     Role           Variable(s)                        Where it renders
#   ─────────── ────────────── ────────────────────────────────── ──────────────────────────
#   Ad-Maven    interstitial   ADMAVEN_INTERSTITIAL_TAG / _URL    between games, ≤1 per 20 min
#   ExoClick    display        EXOCLICK_HEADER_TAG                header 728×90 (320×50 mobile)
#                              EXOCLICK_SIDEBAR_TAG               sidebar 300×250
#   HilltopAds  in-page push   HILLTOPADS_PUSH_SCRIPT             positioned by the network
#   Clickadu    video preroll  CLICKADU_PREROLL_VAST              before the game frame loads
#   Mondiad     native         MONDIAD_NATIVE_TAG                 below the "for you" grids
#   PopAds      popunder       POPADS_POPUNDER_TAG                desktop game pages only
#
#  Plus, for every network:
#      <NETWORK>_ENABLED   1 to turn it on. Unset = renders nothing.
#      <NETWORK>_ZONE      the zone / site ID it issued you (admin panel only)
#
#  STRICT ROLES. A role renders only its owner's code. If the owner is off or
#  has no tag, that placement goes dark — no other network fills in. A variable
#  outside a network's role (EXOCLICK_FOOTER_TAG, HILLTOPADS_SCRIPT, anything for
#  the retired MONETAG_* / ADSTERRA_* networks, the old PROXY_*_TAG slots) is
#  ignored, and its NAME is printed at boot and in the admin panel.
#
#  ENABLED WITHOUT CONFIGURATION RENDERS NOTHING. Both halves are required, so a
#  half-finished setup produces no empty boxes.
#
#  REGIONAL TAGS. Any role variable can be overridden for one region of
#  REGION_ROUTES (see "Regional domains" below) by appending __<REGION>:
#      EXOCLICK_HEADER_TAG__SOUTH_ASIA=<script …>
#  Every field without an override falls back to the global value, so a region
#  with a partial override never loses a placement.
#
#  FIXED, NOT CONFIGURABLE: the interstitial fires at most once per visitor per
#  20 minutes (a constant in public/js/ad-frequency.js), the video pre-roll is
#  skippable at the creative's skip offset (5 seconds by default, which is
#  Clickadu's own rule), and the pre-roll never follows an interstitial that
#  fired within the last 2 minutes. The game always loads, whatever the ad does.
#
#  AUDIENCE. This site is used by minors. In every network dashboard that offers
#  it (ExoClick and Clickadu especially), register the site as MAINSTREAM and
#  exclude adult ad categories.
#
#  START WITH ONE NETWORK. Add the next only once the first is paying.
#
#  ⚠ NO NETWORK HAS APPROVED THIS SITE UNTIL YOU HAVE CONFIRMED IT YOURSELF.
#    Every one of these requires publisher review of your specific domain, and
#    approval for one format does not imply approval for another.
#    Full matrix: docs/features/ADVERTISING.md
#
#  ⚠ ADS NEVER RUN ON THE PROXY. /browser, /gust and every proxy asset route are
#    excluded structurally, in both the server and the client.
#
#  AD-BLOCK GATE. While at least one network is active, a visitor whose blocker
#  stays on for 3 pages finds AnuraOS and the standalone proxy engines locked
#  until they pause it; the games and built-in engines are never locked. Switch
#  it off in the admin console (setting ads.adblock_gate).
#
#  Tags are CREDENTIALS. Anyone holding one can serve ads billed to your account.
#  Set them in your host's dashboard, never in a committed file.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Ad-Maven — interstitial ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# One full-screen unit between game launches, throttled client-side to one per
# visitor per 20 minutes. The throttle fails CLOSED: with no localStorage, no
# interstitial fires. Set the TAG for a rendered creative, or the URL for a
# direct-link — which is only ever offered as a labelled link the visitor may
# ignore, never auto-opened and never a redirect.
# ADMAVEN_ENABLED=1
# ADMAVEN_ZONE=
# ADMAVEN_INTERSTITIAL_TAG=
# ADMAVEN_INTERSTITIAL_URL=

# ── ExoClick — header + sidebar banners ─────────────────────────────────
# Raw zone code, on ONE line. Each box is reserved at full size before the tag
# loads, so a late creative cannot shift the page. The sidebar is hidden below
# 900px wide, where the sidebar collapses.
# EXOCLICK_ENABLED=1
# EXOCLICK_ZONE=
# EXOCLICK_HEADER_TAG=<script async type="application/javascript" src="https://…/ad-provider.js"></script><ins class="…" data-zoneid="…"></ins><script>(AdProvider = window.AdProvider || []).push({"serve": {}});</script>
# EXOCLICK_SIDEBAR_TAG=

# ── HilltopAds — in-page push ────────────────────────────────────────────
# A page-level loader the network positions itself; comma-separated https URLs.
# HILLTOPADS_ENABLED=1
# HILLTOPADS_ZONE=
# HILLTOPADS_PUSH_SCRIPT=https://cdn.example-hilltop.com/in-page-push.js

# ── Clickadu — video pre-roll ────────────────────────────────────────────
# A VAST tag URL (https). IAB macros [CACHEBUSTING] and [TIMESTAMP] are filled
# in. Game pages only. No fill, a blocked tag or a failed video simply lets the
# game load.
# CLICKADU_ENABLED=1
# CLICKADU_ZONE=
# CLICKADU_PREROLL_VAST=https://…/vast?zone=…&cb=[CACHEBUSTING]

# ── Mondiad — native widgets ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Paste the delivery code AND the element code together, on one line. Shown
# under a "Sponsored" label below the "for you" rows, and only loaded once its
# row is visible and near the viewport.
# MONDIAD_ENABLED=1
# MONDIAD_ZONE=
# MONDIAD_NATIVE_TAG=

# ── PopAds — popunder ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PopAds requires its code UNMODIFIED; it is passed through byte for byte.
# Desktop game pages only — never mobile, never hub pages, never a proxy page.
# POPADS_ENABLED=1
# POPADS_ZONE=
# POPADS_POPUNDER_TAG=<script type="text/javascript" data-cfasync="false">/*<![CDATA[/* … PopAds code … */</script>

# Optional extra ads.txt lines (e.g. an MCM partner), \n-separated.
# ADS_TXT_EXTRA=
# Extra ad origins for the Content-Security-Policy. USUALLY NOT NEEDED — the
# script origins in every tag and loader above (including each region's) are
# allowlisted automatically, plus a wildcard for each registrable domain.
# Set this only when a network chains to a DIFFERENT domain and the browser
# console shows "Refused to load the script 'https://…'". Without an allowlist
# entry the browser blocks the script outright, which looks identical to the
# network simply not paying out. Comma-separated, wildcards OK, https only,
# Google ad domains refused. Check with: npm run check-ads
# PROXY_AD_ORIGINS=https://*.someadcdn.com

# ── Legal pages (Privacy / Terms / DMCA / Cookies) ───────────────────────
# Contact shown on the legal pages. If you leave CONTACT_EMAIL blank, the pages
# link to your GitHub repo's Issues page instead. A dedicated inbox is strongly
# recommended for ad-network approval and DMCA notices.
CONTACT_EMAIL=
# Your public site URL, shown in the policy text (e.g. https://zofhub.com).
# If blank, the policies just say "this website".
SITE_URL=

# ── AI (Pollinations) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO KEY, NO SIGNUP, NO COST. The AI chat calls Pollinations' free + keyless
# endpoints DIRECTLY from each visitor's browser:
#   • Text:  https://text.pollinations.ai/…          (openai-fast model)
#   • Image: https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/…  (<img> — works everywhere)
# So AI load never hits this server and there's nothing to configure to make it
# work. (The newer gen.pollinations.ai endpoints now REQUIRE a paid key, so we
# deliberately do NOT use them.)
#
# ONE caveat, dev-only: Pollinations' Cloudflare Turnstile blocks the TEXT fetch
# from http/localhost origins (it keys on the Origin header). On a real https
# domain the keyless text URL works browser-direct; on localhost the app falls
# back to its own /api/ai/text hop so the chat still works while you build. If
# every network route fails, an on-device model answers as a last resort, so the
# chat never hard-fails. Images (via <img>) send no Origin and work everywhere.
#
# Your public site hostname — sent as the referrer for attribution. No secret.
# Use a BARE hostname (no https://); the server adds the scheme. A full URL with
# a scheme is also tolerated (the code strips it), but bare is the canonical form.
POLLINATIONS_REFERRER=your-domain.example
# Optional FREE public key (pk_…) from https://enter.pollinations.ai — lifts the
# anonymous per-IP rate limit. Handed to the browser on purpose, so it must be a
# PUBLIC pk_ key; never put a secret sk_ key here.
# POLLINATIONS_PUBLIC_KEY=
# Optional Pollinations token to lift the per-IP limit (keep secret if set).
# POLLINATIONS_TOKEN=
# Same thing under the name Pollinations' current docs use. If BOTH are set this
# one wins (server.js prefers it), and either one switches the server-side path
# onto the keyed gen.pollinations.ai endpoint. A secret — never a pk_ key.
# POLLINATIONS_API_KEY=
# Optional paid video-generation provider key (Replicate / fal.ai / Luma /
# Runway). Until set, /api/ai/video returns "unavailable" (no free video model).
# VIDEO_API_KEY=
#
# There is no switch that runs AI on this server in production. /api/ai/text,
# /api/ai/image and /api/ai/echo answer 403 whenever NODE_ENV=production, and
# otherwise only to a loopback client (lib/ai-guard.mjs). The escape hatch
# that older builds read (AI_SERVER_FALLBACK) is gone; setting it does nothing.

# ── Advanced tuning (all optional; defaults are in the code) ─────────────
# Extra origins allowed to make cross-origin WRITE requests, comma-separated.
# Reads are open (the catalog is public data); writes are same-origin by default.
# Only add an origin you control — each one can drive write endpoints using its
# own visitors' IPs.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-other-site.example
#
# Largest JSON request body accepted, on every route. Parsing is synchronous, so
# the ceiling bounds how much blocking work one request can buy. Default 100kb —
# far above the largest legitimate body here (an AI-feedback submission).
# JSON_BODY_LIMIT=100kb
#
# Token-bucket rate limits. CAPACITY is the burst size, REFILL_MS the interval at
# which one token returns.
# Each write endpoint now has its OWN independent bucket (lib/rate-limit.mjs);
# there is no single shared WRITE bucket any more. Only the two below are tunable
# from the environment — the rest are sized in code per endpoint.
# AI_RATE_CAPACITY=              # burst allowance for /api/ai/*
# AI_RATE_REFILL_MS=
#
# Live presence, sized for a school/library NAT (hundreds of tabs on one IP).
# The per-address budget is the coarse flood limit; the per-address SESSION cap
# bounds how many players one address can have counted at once.
# PRESENCE_IP_BURST=600           # burst of presence beats per address
# PRESENCE_IP_MS_PER_TOKEN=5      # → 200 beats/sec sustained per address
# PRESENCE_MAX_SESSIONS_PER_IP=500 # distinct tabs one address may have counted
#
# AI pipeline behaviour (lib/ai-pipeline.mjs).
# AI_MODEL_PRIORITY=             # comma-separated model names, best first
# AI_TOTAL_BUDGET_MS=            # wall-clock ceiling for one AI request
# AI_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=
# AI_RETRY_BASE_MS=              # exponential backoff base
# AI_SIDELINE_AFTER=             # consecutive failures before a model is benched
# AI_HEALTH_COOLDOWN_MS=         # how long a benched model stays benched
#
#
# Housekeeping.
# REC_KEEP_MAX=                  # rows the recommendations cron retains
# SKIP_THUMB_FETCH=1             # skip thumbnail backfill at boot (faster start)
#
# Test-suite ports — set only if 3399/3398 collide with something local.
# TEST_PORT=3399
# TEST_PORT_SEC=3398
# CHECK_ADS_BASE=http://localhost:8080   # base URL `npm run check-ads` probes

# ── Optional: Redis cache (falls back to in-memory if unset) ─────────────
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# UPSTASH_REDIS_URL=            # alias — used only if REDIS_URL is unset

# ── Optional: cron control ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# CRON_DISABLED=1   # disable all background jobs (useful for tests / replicas)
# Y8_PAGES=0        # 0 = scrape ALL pages of the Y8 embed directory
# GROW_INTERVAL_HOURS=36  # hours between catalog-grow cycles (the auto-expander)
# GROW_TARGET=30          # render-verified games added per grow cycle

# ── Aggregate quality telemetry ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Counts which games fail to render on which browser families, and bucketed
# Core Web Vitals. There is NO visitor in this data: no IP, no session, no
# device id, no raw user-agent, no raw metric value — only counters keyed by a
# small closed vocabulary (see lib/telemetry.mjs and db/16_game_telemetry.sql).
# Needs db/16_game_telemetry.sql applied to persist; without it the numbers live
# in memory, show in the admin console, and are lost on restart.
# Set to 0 to collect nothing at all — the endpoints then answer 204 and record
# nothing, and the site behaves identically.
# TELEMETRY_ENABLED=1

# ── Keep-alive (free-tier uptime) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Protects GET /healthz (and /ping). Point an external uptime monitor
# (e.g. cron-job.org) at /healthz every ~10 min so Render's free tier never
# sleeps; the endpoint also pokes Supabase so that free-tier DB never pauses.
# Your monitor must send this value as one of:
#   Authorization: Bearer <secret>   |   x-cron-secret: <secret>   |   ?key=<secret>
# Generate a strong random value:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
CRON_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ── sing-box (ON BY DEFAULT) ─────────────────────────────────
# sing-box (github.com/SagerNet/sing-box, GPL-3.0) is a separate Go daemon.
# ZoFHub does not bundle or link it: the Dockerfile builds a pinned, unmodified
# upstream release and this app runs it as its own process. ZoFHub is not
# affiliated with or endorsed by its authors.
#
# WHAT IT DOES HERE. It is the proxy browser's EGRESS. Every TCP stream the
# Wisp tunnel opens for a visitor is dialled through sing-box's loopback SOCKS
# inbound, so it carries the site's real traffic and its management API reports
# real connections. Nothing below needs to be set for that to work.
#
# IT FAILS SOFT. No binary, or a daemon mid-restart, degrades to a direct
# connection rather than breaking the proxy — and the fallback is counted and
# shown in the admin Proxy view, so "degraded" is never silent.

# Turn the egress path off and dial straight out of Node (the pre-sing-box
# behaviour). The daemon may still run and be monitored.
# SINGBOX_EGRESS=0

# Where the daemon's SOCKS/mixed inbound listens. Must match the `listen_port`
# in deploy/singbox/config.template.json.
# SINGBOX_SOCKS=127.0.0.1:2080

# STRICT MODE. Fail a proxied stream outright rather than falling back to a
# direct connection. Choose this when leaking the server's own IP is worse than
# a failed page load; leave it off to prioritise availability.
# SINGBOX_REQUIRE=1

# How long to wait for the SOCKS dial + handshake before giving up on it.
# SINGBOX_DIAL_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# ── Management API (read by the admin panel) ────────────────────
# Defaulted automatically in managed mode: the URL points at the daemon ZoFHub
# starts, and a random secret is generated at boot if none is given. Set these
# only to point at a daemon you run yourself.
#
# BIND THE CONTROLLER TO LOOPBACK. Anyone who reaches that API can rewrite
# routing and read every connection; it must never face the public internet.
# SINGBOX_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9090
# SINGBOX_API_SECRET=              # matches experimental.clash_api.secret
# SINGBOX_API_TIMEOUT_MS=1500      # how long the panel waits for the daemon

# ── Managed mode (ON unless disabled) ────────────────────────
# ZoFHub renders deploy/singbox/config.template.json, validates it with
# `sing-box check`, spawns it, restarts it with backoff if it dies, and kills it
# on shutdown. Set SINGBOX_MANAGED=0 to run the daemon yourself (or not at all)
# and have ZoFHub only read it.
# SINGBOX_MANAGED=0
# SINGBOX_BIN=sing-box                          # or an absolute path
# SINGBOX_CONFIG_PATH=/tmp/zofhub-singbox/config.json   # rendered config (0600)
# SINGBOX_CONFIG_TEMPLATE=                      # override the shipped template

# ── REALITY profile (advanced, NOT the default) ─────────────────
# Only for deploy/singbox/config.reality.template.json, which publishes a
# VLESS+REALITY inbound for native clients. It needs to OWN :443, so it cannot
# run on a platform whose edge terminates TLS. Point SINGBOX_CONFIG_TEMPLATE at
# it and set these. Generate with:
#   sing-box generate reality-keypair   and   sing-box generate uuid
# SINGBOX_UUID_OWNER=
# SINGBOX_HANDSHAKE_HOST=www.example.com        # a real TLS site to borrow
# SINGBOX_REALITY_PRIVATE_KEY=
# SINGBOX_REALITY_SHORT_ID=
#
# Full setup, config templates and deploy shape: docs/proxy/SINGBOX.md

# ── Admin: what the machine token may do ─────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_SECRET is a STATIC bearer token for monitors and CLI use: it never
# expires, has no CSRF protection and is not tied to a person. It therefore runs
# under a role rather than as the owner, so a leaked monitoring token cannot
# revoke admin sessions or purge running config.
#   operator  (default) catalog + moderation + read security. What the README
#                       documents the token as being for.
#   viewer              read-only. The right choice for a pure uptime monitor.
#   content_manager / moderator / analyst / support
#                       the staff roles below admin, for a token scoped to one
#                       job (e.g. a CI job that creates draft games).
# "owner", "super_admin" and "admin" are rejected on purpose and fall back to
# operator: a token in a cron file is not a person, cannot re-authenticate for
# destructive actions, and a typo here must never escalate it.
# ADMIN_SERVICE_ROLE=operator

# ── Client IP: which forwarded hops to trust ─────────────────────────────
# Every per-client control (rate limits, admin lockout, the local-only AI gate)
# keys on the client address, which Express derives from X-Forwarded-For only
# for hops it is told to trust. Getting this wrong is a security bug in BOTH
# directions: too trusting lets a client spoof its address; not trusting the
# real edge collapses every visitor behind it into one bucket.
#   (unset)     production → trust 1 hop (Render's edge); otherwise loopback only
#   1, 2, …     number of proxy hops in front of this app
#   loopback    trust only a proxy on this same machine
#   false / 0   never trust a forwarded header (this app is the edge)
#   CIDRs       e.g. "10.0.0.0/8, 173.245.48.0/20" for a known proxy fleet
# "true" is refused — it would make every client address spoofable.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# ── Health check: memory ceiling ─────────────────────────────────────────
# GET /healthz returns 503 once memory use crosses this percentage, so an
# orchestrator can replace the container BEFORE the OOM killer takes it out
# mid-request. The reported figure is the worse of (RSS / container limit) and
# (V8 used heap / heap limit) — either one can kill the process.
# Anything outside 1-100 is treated as a typo and the 85 default is used.
# HEALTH_MEM_MAX_PCT=85
#
# A breach must PERSIST for this long before /healthz and /readyz flip to 503,
# so a one-off GC pause or a single large parse cannot recycle a healthy
# instance. It flips back the moment a sample is under the line. Default 10s.
# HEALTH_SUSTAIN_MS=10000
#
# /readyz also fails when the event loop is stalled: the p99 delay over a 5s
# window, in ms. A stalled loop means the process cannot serve even though it is
# alive. Default 1000.
# HEALTH_EVENT_LOOP_P99_MS=1000
#
# /livez is liveness only (503 just after an uncaught exception); /readyz and
# /healthz are readiness. Render's one health check points at /healthz.

# ── Proxy tunnels: per-client fairness cap ───────────────────────────────
# The Wisp tunnel has a hard global ceiling (512 concurrent, in code). This is
# the PER-CLIENT slice of that pool: one IP cannot hold more than this many
# tunnels at once, so a runaway tab or a script on one visitor's end cannot
# starve everyone else. Generous on purpose — a shared NAT counts as one client
# — because it exists to stop a single leak, not to ration normal use. Default 32.
# WISP_MAX_PER_IP=32

# ── Advanced: companion proxy browsers ───────────────────────────────────
# ZoFHub can list separately-run standalone proxy browsers in the /browser
# engine picker. These are OPTIONAL and NOT bundled (see docs/CREDITS.md) — point
# them at the services only if you actually run them; leave unset to hide them.
# Setting any ONE of these is enough to make that engine appear in the dropdown
# (whenever it's reachable) — the server is the single source of truth, and the
# browser page lists whatever /api/proxies returns. `npm run dev:all` sets all
# four automatically, so for local use you normally don't need to touch these.
# Defaults shown are the bundled-launcher ports.
# PROXY_NAUTILUS_URL=http://localhost:8091/
# PROXY_INTERSTELLAR_URL=http://localhost:8092/
# PROXY_DAYDREAM_URL=http://localhost:8093/
# PROXY_NEBULA_URL=http://localhost:8094/
#
# PROXY_INVISI_URL — InvisiProxy LTS (AGPL-3.0, formerly Holy Unblocker LTS).
#   NOT bundled and NOT startable by `npm run proxies`: it is a separate
#   application on Fastify + pnpm. Run it from its own checkout
#   (`pnpm install && pnpm start`, Node >= 22) and point this at it.
#   ZoFHub ships none of its code — see docs/proxy/INVISIPROXY.md for why, and for
#   the two ideas that WERE adopted from reviewing it.
# PROXY_INVISI_URL=https://invisi.example.com/
#
# ⚠ NEVER put a password in any PROXY_*_URL. /api/proxies publishes these
#   URLs to every visitor, unauthenticated. Credentials are stripped before
#   that happens and a warning is logged at boot — treat that as a backstop,
#   not a feature.
#
# ── Regional domains (lib/geo.mjs) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Keeps each region's visitors on that region's own domain(s), so one market's
# block lists stop reaching another market's domain; and lets any ad variable
# have a per-region value (see ADVERTISING above). Unset = one audience, one
# domain set, exactly as before.
#
# REGION_ROUTES — regions separated by ';', each ID:COUNTRIES[=HOST,HOST…]
#   ID         letters, digits, _ — the suffix for regional ad variables
#   COUNTRIES  ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 codes; `*` = the one catch-all region
#   HOSTS      https domains serving that region, first one canonical. Omit
#              them to use the region for ad localisation only.
# Only top-level page loads are redirected (302) — never API calls, assets,
# frames, health checks, ads.txt or legal pages. A visitor with a MASQR key
# carries it across — which works only if every regional domain's deployment
# uses the SAME MASQR_SECRET (automatic when they are custom domains on one
# service). `?geo=stay` opts a visitor out for seven days. Everything is parsed
# once at boot; a request costs two Map lookups and no memory.
# REGION_ROUTES=SOUTH_ASIA:IN,PK,BD,LK,NP,BT,MV=play-in.example.com;NORTH_AMERICA:US,CA=play-us.example.com;GLOBAL:*=zofhub.example.com
#
# GEO_COUNTRY_HEADER — the header your EDGE sets with the visitor's country.
# Default cf-ipcountry (Cloudflare, which overwrites any client-sent value).
# Without a geo-aware edge in front, the client controls this header — the
# worst it can do is choose its own region.
# GEO_COUNTRY_HEADER=cf-ipcountry

# ── MASQR: proxy discovery gate (docs/proxy/MASQR.md) ──────────────────────────
# OFF by default. Turning it on means an unauthenticated visitor — including a
# filter vendor's crawler — sees only the games hub, and the proxy surfaces
# answer 404. A key, handed out in a link, unlocks them.
#
# WHY: a mirror domain usually dies because somebody shares the link, it reaches
# a filter vendor, their crawler fetches it, sees a web proxy, and the domain is
# burned. Mirror rotation is the cure; this is the prevention.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS NOT ACCESS CONTROL. A key holder can pass the key on, and the games
#   hub stays open to everyone regardless. It buys discovery resistance against
#   automated classifiers, nothing more.
#
# Never gated, whatever you set: /healthz (gating it restart-loops the
# container), the legal pages, /licenses, /robots.txt, and the whole games hub.
#
# MASQR_ENABLED — 1 turns the gate on. Anything else leaves the site exactly as
#   it is today.
# MASQR_ENABLED=0
#
# MASQR_SECRET — signs the keys. REQUIRED when enabled, 16+ characters; without
#   it the gate cannot verify anything, so it refuses to enable and says so at
#   boot rather than denying every visitor. Generate one with:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#   Rotating it invalidates every outstanding key at once — that is the only
#   revocation there is, because keys are signed rather than stored.
# MASQR_SECRET=
#
# MASQR_KEY_TTL_DAYS — default lifetime of a minted key. Default 30.
# MASQR_KEY_TTL_DAYS=30
#
# MASQR_DENY — what an ungated visitor gets on a proxy path: "404" (default,
#   most classification-resistant) or "redirect" to the games hub.
# MASQR_DENY=404
# MASQR_REDIRECT_TO=/
#
# Mint keys as an admin: POST /api/admin/masqr/key {"days": 7}
#
# ── Wisp egress policy (the proxy's security boundary) ───────────────────
# /wisp/ is the one endpoint here that opens a TCP connection to an address a
# stranger chose, so these are the rules that decide what this site can be
# used for. Defaults are safe; every widening is warned about at boot and
# reported in /api/admin/proxy. Full reasoning: docs/proxy/INVISIPROXY.md §2b.
#
# WISP_ALLOWED_PORTS — ports the tunnel may dial. Default 80,443,8080,8443.
#   Accepts ranges ("8000-8100") and the literal "any" to disable the check.
#   The default deliberately excludes 25 (mail relay), 22, 3306, 5432, 6379.
# WISP_ALLOWED_PORTS=80,443,8080,8443
#
# WISP_ALLOW_UDP — default off. UDP streams do NOT pass through sing-box
#   (only TCPSocket is injected), so SINGBOX_REQUIRE does not cover them.
#   Nothing in ZoFHub uses UDP through the tunnel.
# WISP_ALLOW_UDP=0
#
# WISP_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS / WISP_ALLOW_LOOPBACK_IPS — default off, and they
#   should stay off. Enabling either turns the public tunnel into a route to
#   this deployment's private network and to 169.254.169.254.
# WISP_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=0
# WISP_ALLOW_LOOPBACK_IPS=0
#
# WISP_MAX_STREAMS — concurrent streams ONE tunnel may hold. Default 256.
#   WISP_MAX_PER_IP caps tunnels; this caps what each one can open.
# WISP_MAX_STREAMS=256
#
# WISP_BLOCKED_HOSTS — comma-separated host globs, anchored:
#   "*.evil.net,ads.example.com". A "*" matches one label, not many.
# WISP_BLOCKED_HOSTS=
#
# ── Proxy governance: approved destinations (docs/admin/CONTROL-PLANE.md) ──
# The tunnel connects ONLY to destinations an administrator approved in the
# console (Proxy control → Approved destinations). With none approved, every
# destination is refused — the proxy fails closed, never open.
#
# PROXY_APPROVED_TARGETS — destinations approved from the environment, for a
#   deployment without the database or as a floor that always applies. Comma-
#   separated hostnames or *.domain wildcards, optional ports after a colon
#   joined by "/" (default 443). Public suffixes (*.com, *.co.uk) and wildcard-
#   DNS services (nip.io, sslip.io, …) are refused and reported at boot.
# PROXY_APPROVED_TARGETS=example.org, *.wikipedia.org:443/80
#
# PROXY_ACCESS_MODE — who may use the proxy until a policy is saved in the
#   console: public (default, approved destinations only), staff (signed-in
#   staff holding proxy.use), or disabled (no tunnels, engine pages answer 503).
# PROXY_ACCESS_MODE=public
#
# PROXY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS — extra page origins allowed to open /wisp/ tunnels, for
#   a mirror served from another hostname. The site's own origin is always
#   allowed; any other Origin is refused so another website cannot use this
#   server's tunnel from its visitors' browsers.
# PROXY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://mirror.example.org

# ── Control plane: local development seed ────────────────────────────────
# CONTROL_MEMORY_SEED — path to a JSON file of { settings, targets, games } that
#   the IN-MEMORY control plane starts with. Honoured only when no database is
#   configured, so it can never touch real data. Used by the test suite.
# CONTROL_MEMORY_SEED=

# ── AnuraOS (AGPL-3.0) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A browser-based web OS with x86 emulation, not just a proxy. Unlike the four
# above, ZoFHub can serve it ITSELF — so normally you set none of these:
#
#   npm run fetch-anura
#
# and it appears in the /browser engine picker on the next start. It is served
# on an origin of its OWN (a second port), because its bundle uses
# root-absolute paths that also name the emulated machine's guest filesystem
# (/usr, /tmp, /boot) — rehosting it under a subpath corrupts the Linux image
# inside it. Without the bundle fetched, no entry appears at all.
#
# ANURA_PORT — the second listener. Default 8095. Set 0 to turn it off.
#   A different port is already a different origin, so this needs no DNS. It
#   does need the port to be reachable, which is fine locally but not on a
#   host that exposes only one (Render) — there, use ANURA_HOST instead.
# ANURA_PORT=8095
#
# ANURA_HOST — serve it on the MAIN listener for one hostname. Point a
#   subdomain at this same service and name it here. This is the production
#   shape: one port, one deployment, two origins.
# ANURA_HOST=anura.example.com
#
# PROXY_ANURA_URL — somebody else's deployment. Overrides both of the above
#   and ZoFHub serves nothing itself.
# PROXY_ANURA_URL=https://anura.example.com/
#
# ── Kasm Workspaces (optional remote desktop) ────────────────────────────
# A real Linux desktop or app in a container, streamed by KasmVNC. Different in
# kind from everything above: Scramjet and Ultraviolet rewrite pages, AnuraOS
# emulates x86 in your own tab, and this one is somebody else's actual computer.
#
# ZoFHub CANNOT host this and no setting will make it. Upstream ships
# Dockerfiles, a session needs --shm-size=512m and gigabytes of RAM, and a
# Render web service has no Docker daemon inside it. You run the container; this
# only points at it. See docs/proxy/KASM.md.
#
#   docker run --rm -it --shm-size=512m -p 6901:6901 \
#     -e VNC_PW=yourpassword kasmweb/desktop:1.19.0
#
# PROXY_KASM_URL — where that container is answering. Unset = no entry at all.
#
#   ⚠ THE CONTAINER'S CERTIFICATE IS SELF-SIGNED. It serves https only (-sslOnly)
#     with a certificate it generates at startup, so a browser refuses to load it
#     inside a frame and reports nothing when it does. ZoFHub detects this and
#     tells the visitor to open it in a tab and accept the certificate once,
#     instead of showing a frame that will never fill. Put it behind a real
#     certificate (a reverse proxy with Let's Encrypt) and it embeds normally.
#
#   ⚠ DO NOT PUT A PASSWORD IN THIS URL. /api/proxies publishes it to every
#     visitor, unauthenticated. Credentials here are stripped before that
#     happens and a warning is logged at boot, but do not rely on that — sign in
#     at Kasm itself. A private address (192.168.x.x, localhost) is accepted and
#     labelled as such, since only that network can reach it.
# PROXY_KASM_URL=https://kasm.example.com:6901/
#
# KASM_LABEL — what to call it in the engine picker. These images range from a
#   full Ubuntu desktop to a single browser, so the default "Kasm Workspace" is
#   often not what you are actually running.
# KASM_LABEL=Kasm Desktop
#
# NOTE: these are LOCAL-ONLY unless you deploy each companion as its own web
# service. They're whole standalone apps that assume they own the site root, so
# they cannot be mounted under a ZoFHub sub-path. On the live deploy none of
# these are set, so /browser shows the three built-in same-origin engines
# (Scramjet · Ultraviolet · GUST). To get a companion live, deploy it somewhere
# and set its URL here to that public address — no code change needed.
# Matching *_PORT variants (PROXY_NAUTILUS_PORT, PROXY_INTERSTELLAR_PORT,
# PROXY_DAYDREAM_PORT, PROXY_NEBULA_PORT) are also read by the bundled launcher.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DONATIONS  (all optional — unset means the donate dialog simply has nothing
# to show, which is the correct default for a fresh deployment)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Receiving addresses. NOT committed to git: a committed address is bound to
# this repository's history forever and cannot be un-published by rotating it.
# Each is checksum-verified at boot and DROPPED with a logged reason if wrong,
# so a typo shows up as a missing button rather than as lost donations.
# XMR and SOL are SHAPE-CHECKED ONLY, not checksum-verified: Monero uses
# Keccak-256 (which node:crypto lacks) and a Solana address is a raw Ed25519
# public key with no checksum in the format at all. On those two a typo can pass
# validation and silently swallow every donation — send yourself a test
# transaction on both before announcing them.
DONATE_SOL_ADDRESS=
DONATE_BTC_ADDRESS=
DONATE_LTC_ADDRESS=
DONATE_ETH_ADDRESS=
DONATE_XMR_ADDRESS=
# DONATE_ETH_ADDRESS must be the MIXED-CASE (EIP-55) form your wallet shows.
# An all-lowercase 0x address carries no checksum at all, so it is rejected
# rather than accepted unverified — paste the address exactly as MetaMask,
# Ledger or Etherscan displays it. The same address receives on Ethereum,
# Base, Arbitrum and Optimism.
# The Solana address above receives USDC (and any other SPL token) as well as
# SOL — one address, both options, no extra configuration.
#
# ARRR (Pirate Chain) and FIRO were removed deliberately: ARRR is very hard for
# a donor to obtain, and FIRO is transparent unless the sender opts into
# Lelantus Spark, so it offered nothing Litecoin did not. ETH is absent because
# L1 gas routinely exceeds the size of a donation this site receives.

# Swap donations: donor pays BTC/LTC/SOL/ETH/DOGE, provider converts to XMR and
# forwards to DONATE_XMR_ADDRESS. Requires DONATE_XMR_ADDRESS to be set.
# Leave SWAP_PROVIDER empty to keep this off entirely.
SWAP_PROVIDER=
# Optional. SideShift works without one at a worse rate and tighter limits.
# SECRET — never commit a real value here.
SIDESHIFT_SECRET=
SIDESHIFT_AFFILIATE_ID=

# Egress proxy for outbound payment API calls. Honoured only when the process
# is started with --use-env-proxy (Node 24+, built in, no dependency):
#     node --use-env-proxy server.js
# This is an HTTP CONNECT proxy. Tor's default 9050 is SOCKS5 and will NOT
# work; add `HTTPTunnelPort 9080` to torrc and point this at 127.0.0.1:9080.
# Be aware: swap providers commonly block Tor exits and geo-restrict, so this
# can turn into donations silently failing. It also hides the server's IP from
# the provider without changing what the provider records — they still receive
# the destination Monero address on every request.
HTTPS_PROXY=
